But what if between calls, the buffer was reallocated and moved? `realloc` preserves the content, so `FD_BUF[FD_LINE_END]` still points to the same logical position. This is fine.

OK, let me look at the `ps_io_fgetchars` function's handling of the `FD_BUF[FD_LINE_END] = FD_LINE_TERMCHAR` line when `FD_LINE_END` is beyond the current `FD_BUF_END`:

After `ps_io_fseek`:
```c
FD_BUF_END = FD_LINE_BEGIN = FD_LINE_END = FD_LINE_LEN = 0;
```

So `FD_LINE_END` is 0. Fine.

After `ps_io_rewind`:
```c
FD_BUF_END = 0;
FD_LINE_BEGIN = 0;
FD_LINE_END = 0;
FD_LINE_LEN = 0;
FD_LINE_TERMCHAR = '\0';
```

So `FD_LINE_END` is 0 and `FD_LINE_TERMCHAR` is '\0'. Fine.

OK, I think the vulnerability might be in the `ps_io_fgetchars` function's handling of the buffer when `realloc` is called and the new memory is used by `fread`, but `fread` returns fewer bytes than `LINE_CHUNK_SIZE`. The bytes between the end of the `fread` data and the end of the `LINE_CHUNK_SIZE` chunk are uninitialized. But `FD_BUF[FD_BUF_END] = '\0'` is set after `fread`, so the buffer is null-terminated.

Wait, but what if `FD_BUF_END` overflows? `FD_BUF_END` is `int`. If `fread` returns a large value, `FD_BUF_END` could overflow. But `fread` returns at most `LINE_CHUNK_SIZE` (4096), so `FD_BUF_END` can't overflow from a single `fread`. And the while loop ensures that `FD_BUF_SIZE` doesn't exceed `MAX_PS_IO_FGETCHARS_BUF_SIZE` (57344), so `FD_BUF_END` can't exceed 57344.

OK, I think I need to look at this from a completely different angle. Let me look at the `spectre_document_load_from_stream` function and the `document_load` function:

```c
static void
document_load (SpectreDocument *document,
               const char      *filename,
               FILE            *file)
{
    ...
    document->doc = psscan (file, filename, SCANSTYLE_NORMAL);
    if (!document->doc) {
        document->status = SPECTRE_STATUS_LOAD_ERROR;
        return;
    }

    if (document->doc->numpages == 0 && document->doc->lenprolog == 0) {
        document->status = SPECTRE_STATUS_LOAD_ERROR;
        psdocdestroy (document->doc);
        document->doc = NULL;
        return;
    } else if (document->doc->numpages == 0 && !document->doc->format) {
        unsigned char *data = NULL;
        int            row_length;
        spectre_document_render (document, &data, &row_length);
        free (data);
        if (spectre_document_status (document)) {
            document->status = SPECTRE_STATUS_LOAD_ERROR;
            psdocdestroy (document->doc);
            document->doc = NULL;
            return;
        }
    }
    ...
}
```

If `psscan` returns a document with `numpages == 0` and `format == NULL`, the code tries to render. The rendering calls `spectre_gs_process` which sends data to Ghostscript. But Ghostscript is not installed, so this fails.

But wait, what if `psscan` returns a document with `numpages == 0` and `format != NULL`? Then the document is considered valid without rendering. The fuzzer then checks `spectre_document_status(document)` and returns.

So the vulnerability must be triggered during `psscan`. Let me look at `psscan` more carefully for potential uninitialized memory reads.

Let me look at the `readline` function's handling of `%%BeginDocument`:

```c
if  (!IS_COMMENT("Begin"))     {} /* Do nothing */
else if IS_BEGIN("Document:")  {
    nesting_level=1;
    line = ps_io_fgetchars(fd,-1);
    if (line) *line_lenP += FD_LINE_LEN;
    while (line) {
        if (IS_COMMENT("Begin") && IS_BEGIN("Document:"))
            nesting_level++;
        else if (IS_COMMENT("End") && IS_END("Document"))
            nesting_level--;
        if (nesting_level == 0) break;
        line = ps_io_fgetchars(fd,-1);
        if (line) *line_lenP += FD_LINE_LEN;
    }
}
```

This reads lines until the nesting level reaches 0. The data is from the file, not uninitialized.

